{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A deliberate design is not a natural monopoly, and the cost of internal coordination in a large system has no natural equivalent in a federated protocol."],"overturn_conditions":["A formal model shows that system-wide cohesion in large services can be maintained without correlated failures, or that decentralized networks cannot be made to pay higher independence costs."],"shared_structure":["Both antitrust and network resilience involve a tradeoff between efficiency and the risk of correlated collapse.","Both require a 'merger policy' that prevents a single entity from optimizing so tightly that it endangers the whole system."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["Just as antitrust regulators should look beyond market-share heuristics to assess systemic risk, network architects should look beyond node-count heuristics to assess failure independence.","The evidence that a merger creates 'unobservable correlated risk' in economics mirrors the evidence that a centralized service creates 'unobservable correlated risk' in computing."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is not inherently more resilient; its resilience depends entirely on the cost of maintaining true independence, which can be measured by the absence of shared dependencies, whereas a large service can achieve resilience by paying the internal cost of diversity.","counterconditions":["If a large service can achieve perfect internal diversity, the coordination cost of a decentralized network becomes pure overhead.","If all decentralized nodes adopt identical software stacks, the large service with a diverse stack is strictly more resilient.","If the correlation length of a shared external signal exceeds the size of the network, independence of operation is irrelevant."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independence of operation is not independence of failure. The large service can internally subsidize diversity (e.g., mandatory different implementations for redundant paths), whereas the decentralized network must coordinate and pay a diversity premium externally. Unless the decentralized network pays this coordination cost, it will converge on a monoculture.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea","66e72c34fa4f21bed07037389788299ee7e94a7113872a5dc83e9f0fc908d157"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["de2b2d90317d6e729fee0a7c23797d72e4fe60dc85fb3870a68bcbb248bd08ef","b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are strictly more fragile to correlated failure when operators independently optimize, because the same objective function causes them to converge on identical solutions, creating an algorithmic monoculture that fails in synchrony.","counterconditions":["If operators have non-aligned incentives, they will not converge.","If operators use different optimization algorithms, the monoculture does not arise.","If a large service uses the same optimization algorithm internally, it suffers the same monoculture."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independent operators facing the same environment and metrics will independently discover the same optimal configuration. This creates a shared cause of failure that is not apparent until the environment changes, at which point all operators fail simultaneously because they all made the same locally-rational but globally-fragile choice.","optional_refs":["78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056","b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea","78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A flight has a pilot in control, whereas decentralized networks are often uncontrolled.","Pilot fatigue is a physiological state, whereas market signals are information."],"overturn_conditions":["If decentralized operators do not react to the same signals, or if a large service is shown to have no equivalent 'attention' bottleneck."],"shared_structure":["Both systems are subject to a common external rhythm that synchronizes the attention of independent units.","In both cases, the synchronization of attention causes correlated failure when the environment changes abruptly."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["Just as multiple pilots are not independent if they all suffer fatigue at the same point in the circadian cycle, multiple services are not independent if they all react to the same market signal.","The evidence of circadian fatigue is a shared rhythm in errors; the evidence of algorithmic convergence is a shared rhythm in scaling decisions."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The true measure of correlated failure is the information-theoretic distance between the decision functions of the operators; a large service with a higher internal diversity of decision functions can be more resilient than a decentralized network with a lower diversity.","counterconditions":["If a large service enforces a single decision function, it is as fragile as the decentralized network.","If the decentralized network maintains a high diversity of decision functions, it is more resilient.","If the large service's internal diversity is merely nominal and all paths share a common mode, the diversity is illusory."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Resilience is a function of the diversity of responses to a given input. A decentralized network of operators using the same auto-scaling algorithm has a lower diversity of responses than a large service that can enforce the use of different algorithms. The shared cause is the optimization model.","optional_refs":["109eed353bebfbbe11b08ebb5abe991b86b13f48a4446d7adc364321a4087bc9","78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056","109eed353bebfbbe11b08ebb5abe991b86b13f48a4446d7adc364321a4087bc9"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Stress is an internal physiological state, whereas market signals are external information.","There is no 'recovery' from a market signal; the information is simply consumed."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'hysteresis' of the large service is shown to be a source of fragility rather than resilience, or if the decentralized network has no such hysteresis."],"shared_structure":["Both systems exhibit a non-linear response to a common input, where the response is determined by the history of prior inputs.","In both cases, the shared history creates a 'memory' that causes the system to fail when the input changes, even if the components are independent."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["The evidence of a 'stress memory' in a large service is a delay in its response to a new input; the evidence of an 'optimization memory' in a decentralized network is a delay in its adaptation to a new environment.","In both cases, the memory is a shared cause of failure that is not apparent until the input changes."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Evidence distinguishing truly independent failures from uncorrelated ones is found in the joint distribution of failure times under controlled stress: truly independent failures will not synchronize as stress increases, whereas uncorrelated failures will synchronize as they approach a shared breaking point.","counterconditions":["If the breaking point is not reached, the failures will not synchronize.","If the large service has a different breaking point than the decentralized network, the comparison is invalid.","If the stress is not applied uniformly, the synchronization may be due to the stress, not the system."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Uncorrelated failures are independent until a shared stress exceeds a common threshold. As stress increases, the failure rate increases, but the failures remain uncorrelated if they are truly independent. If they are merely uncorrelated, the shared stress will cause them to synchronize as the threshold is approached.","optional_refs":["61fbe5cbe92530d7b01a2f864e2e74a3f9c16191b60abe989139bcfdf86f9ead","66e72c34fa4f21bed07037389788299ee7e94a7113872a5dc83e9f0fc908d157"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["61fbe5cbe92530d7b01a2f864e2e74a3f9c16191b60abe989139bcfdf86f9ead","66e72c34fa4f21bed07037389788299ee7e94a7113872a5dc83e9f0fc908d157"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A large service has a single designer, whereas a decentralized network has many.","The 'redundancy' in a large service is explicit, whereas in a decentralized network it is implicit."],"overturn_conditions":["If a large service is shown to be unable to achieve true internal diversity, or if a decentralized network is shown to be unable to avoid external convergence."],"shared_structure":["Both antitrust and network resilience involve a tradeoff between efficiency and diversity.","Both require a 'competition policy' that prevents a single entity from optimizing so tightly that it endangers the whole system."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["Just as antitrust regulators should look beyond market-share heuristics to assess systemic risk, network architects should look beyond node-count heuristics to assess failure independence.","The evidence that a merger creates 'unobservable correlated risk' in economics mirrors the evidence that a centralized service creates 'unobservable correlated risk' in computing."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is always more fragile than a large service because it lacks a central authority to enforce diversity; without enforcement, the network will always converge to a monoculture, whereas the large service can be mandated to maintain diversity.","counterconditions":["If the decentralized network has a social or cultural mechanism to enforce diversity, it can be resilient.","If the large service is incentivized to cut costs by reducing diversity, it will become fragile.","If the 'diversity' is not in the relevant dimension, it does not improve resilience."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Diversity is not the default; it is a costly investment. A large service can internalize the cost of diversity and mandate it as a design requirement. A decentralized network has no such authority; each operator optimizes locally, and the global result is a monoculture. This is a structural advantage of the large service.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","964cdafe2c6002c79c52c87a912ee05f9a85b89bd845cab193fd3604687b167d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","964cdafe2c6002c79c52c87a912ee05f9a85b89bd845cab193fd3604687b167d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.25},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Social capital is built through trust, whereas network independence is built through isolation.","The 'coupling' in a social network is often positive, whereas in a computer network it is often negative."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'social capital' of the large service is shown to be a source of fragility, or if the decentralized network has no such capital."],"shared_structure":["Both systems are more resilient if their components are 'loosely coupled' and can fail independently.","In both cases, the 'coupling' is not apparent until a failure propagates."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["The evidence of 'weak ties' in a social network is the ability of the network to maintain connectivity after a node is removed; the evidence of 'weak coupling' in a computer network is the ability of the network to maintain functionality after a service fails.","In both cases, the evidence is a measure of the network's redundancy, not of the independence of its components."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The resilience of a network is inversely proportional to its correlation length; a decentralized network has a shorter correlation length than a large service, but only if the correlation length is actively managed, which is a cost the large service can pay more easily.","counterconditions":["If the correlation length is not managed, the decentralized network will have the same correlation length as the large service.","If the large service can internally segment its correlation length, it can be as resilient as the decentralized network.","If the correlation length is not the relevant measure of resilience, the comparison is invalid."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The correlation length is the distance over which a failure in one component increases the probability of failure in another. A large service can internally segment its components to reduce the correlation length, but it must pay the cost of maintaining the segments. A decentralized network has a shorter correlation length by default, but it must pay the cost of maintaining the independence.","optional_refs":["78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056","b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056","b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.15}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["de2b2d90317d6e729fee0a7c23797d72e4fe60dc85fb3870a68bcbb248bd08ef","b4500dccf853bcf6fdf31585870f10f8c7b5efc1d110e96c915ff087162089ea","78804b8d7b769c4637f6c5b551cc8b86242f087658f196d34868ec970e552056","109eed353bebfbbe11b08ebb5abe991b86b13f48a4446d7adc364321a4087bc9","61fbe5cbe92530d7b01a2f864e2e74a3f9c16191b60abe989139bcfdf86f9ead","66e72c34fa4f21bed07037389788299ee7e94a7113872a5dc83e9f0fc908d157","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","964cdafe2c6002c79c52c87a912ee05f9a85b89bd845cab193fd3604687b167d"]},"simulation_proposals":[]}